WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.560
CRISPR, the molecular scissors

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.520
that shot to fame in 2012,

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.380
has revolutionized biology because it makes it simpler

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.860
and cheaper for scientists to cut genomes

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.520
wherever they chose.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.320
The power of CRISPR lies in what happens

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:24.779
after it makes a cut.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.240
Theoretically, the natural system that repairs cuts

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.480
can cripple disease-causing genes.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.140
Or scientists can swap in new DNA

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.100
potentially fixing, say, the mutation

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.540
that causes sickle-cell anemia.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.320
Recently, several labs have manipulated

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.960
the CRISPR platform for diagnostics --

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.620
to simplify detection of viruses or even tumors.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:50.980
CRISPR complex finds a targeted part of a genome

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.080
with a guide RNA.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.570
The guide tows along an enzyme from what’s known

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.760
as the Cas family that does the actual cutting.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.660
But Cas enzymes come in many varieties.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.280
after they make the targeted cut,

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.640
Some go into a feeding frenzy and cut any

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.260
single stranded nucleic acids nearby.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.260
These are the Cas enzymes used in detection systems.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.040
Imagine blood from a patient suspected of having Zika.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.700
Basically, researchers could prep the sample,

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.780
add all the necessary ingredients, and then spike it

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.360
with a piece of single stranded DNA or RNA

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.940
that has a fluorescent reporter system attached to it.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:35.640
If CRISPR finds Zika,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.600
The Cas enzyme cuts the virus

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.100
and cuts the molecule holding the reporter,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.300
which makes it fluoresce.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.240
The latest versions of these systems,

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.460
nicknamed SHERLOCK and DECTECTR,

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.820
have shown that they simultaneously can find

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.540
several different viruses

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.600
or mutations associated with tumors.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.640
The researchers also have made progress

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.300
on moving CRISPR based detections to test strips,

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.720
similar to what’s used in pregnancy tests.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.980
One day, they envision it may be possible

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:10.780
with just a drop of blood,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.660
to have an easy-to-read result

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.420
that tells doctors whether a patient has Zika virus

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.680
or its close cousin Dengue.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.860
A multi-channeled strip may also reveal

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.660
which type of dengue.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.680
First genome editing, now diagnostics--

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.940
where will CRISPR take us next?

